@gusvianadev I see the issue! You're using the workaround approach, but looking at my working config, the Figma MCP actually works directly as a remote server with OAuth. Here's an alternative you might want to try:

```json
{
  "figma": {
    "type": "remote",
    "url": "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp",
    "timeout": 60000,
    "oauth": {}
  }
}
```

You don't actually need to do the MCP remote workaround for Figma. It supports remote type directly with OAuth authentication. It's probably because mcp-remote is trying to proxy a connection that Figma expects to handle directly through the OAuth flow.

Just remove the workaround config and use the direct remote connection. OpenCode will take care of the OAuth flow for you when you first connect.

If you also want the Figma Desktop integration (for local files), you can add this separately:

```json
{
  "figma-desktop": {
    "type": "remote",
    "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3845/mcp",
    "timeout": 30000
  }
}
```

But start with the main Figma remote config. Then you can decide whether you want to use Figma Desktop as well.

@gusvianadev, I guess that the 405 error you're encountering on Linux is due to Figma MCP requiring a streamable HTTP transport and a personal access token instead of OAuth. Here’s the new configuration you could try:

```json
{
  "figma": {
    "type": "local",
    "command": "npx",
    "args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp", "--transport", "streamable-http"],
    "timeout": 120000,
    "env": {
      "FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN": "your-figma-personal-access-token"
    }
  }
}
```

And to get the PAT, go to figma.com, check "Personal Access Tokens" under your account settings, and create a new one there.

Streamable HTTP (not HTTP-only) and token authentication should bypass the Linux OAuth issues.

+1 for having this issue with opencode `1.1.17` in both CLI and Desktop while using an Azure provider.

Here's my opencode config as it was requested above. 
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "share": "disabled",
  "autoupdate": true,
  "provider": {
    "azure-cognitive-services": {
      "models": {
        "devteam-gpt-5.1-codex": {
          "name": "devteam-gpt-5.1-codex",
          "options": {
            "store": true
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Can also confirm the following command does work
> Like `opencode agent create --model opencode/big-pickle`
